## Context

The user runs agent sessions in several projects at once, mostly inside subagents the terminal barely shows. They need one trustworthy place that says, per project, which session is working, in which pipeline and phase, and whether it waits on them, and that replays what past sessions did. Two agent sessions in one project overwrite each other's specs, plans and commits, so the same place must keep them from running together.

Four facts shape the design. The pipeline's current state already lives in the project's files, which record their own status and progress; anything derived elsewhere can only disagree. What the files lack (who ran a phase, when, from which prompt, in what order, for which pipeline) exists only as the agent reports it, and reports are best effort. An agent cannot report its own crash or closed terminal, but Claude Code runs hooks by itself, apart from the aide server, at every session start, prompt, tool call, stop and session end; other hosts have none. And the project's repo must stay clean of anything the observer keeps.

A session waiting an hour on the user still holds the project; a session that exited does not. Judging that from report age once counted an exited session live for half an hour. The board lives in a brain whose backend and layout vary, so its location cannot be guessed from the launcher or the backend; the project's brain config declares it.

## Strategy

**Session start is the one gate.** The agent first reports the session start, whose reply names its observer session, then calls start. Start reuses the machine's one observer or launches it, trying the user's port, then a short fixed list in order, and records the port taken so tools and links find it. If no port works, the agent tells the user and nothing proceeds: a session the observer cannot see breaks its single-source promise.

**One live session per project, decided from stored presence.** Start then checks presence and ended marks on disk, not an observer that may have replayed nothing, for any other open session in the project, whether or not it ever reported its start, since it can edit files all the same. If one is found, start halts naming that session, its state, last activity, pipeline and phase. Nothing proceeds until the user approves ending it, only a mark and never the caller's own, or closes this session.

**Presence comes from the host, not the agent.** Claude Code's hooks mark a session working on start, prompt and tool call, waiting when the agent stops, and ended when the session ends. Each call writes presence first, keyed by the host's session and joined to the observer session when the hook on the session-start report reads its reply, keeps the transcript's location, then pings a running observer, never failing the agent. Hosts without hooks fall back to reported events.

**Liveness is judged, never acted on.** An ended mark means ended; otherwise the last hook state holds, and last activity is the newest of the hook's time, the transcript's last write and the newest report. Past 15 quiet minutes it reads quiet and maybe stuck, never ended: a session waiting on the user still holds the project, and only its end hook or the user's approval releases it. Process ids are never consulted; they say nothing about whether the agent holds its work.

**Record first, then notify; a down observer gets one restart.** Every report lands in its session's own log outside the project, presence and ended marks kept beside it so the log has one writer, then is posted to a running observer. A missed post never blocks by itself but prompts one restart; a restart that finds no port blocks mid-session too. A report for an ended session is refused, so a released session cannot write on as if it held the project.

**Pipelines are reported explicitly; rails resume from kept logs.** A pipeline is one feature flow from its starting stage to completion or abandonment, named by its board task when it has one. The agent reports its start, finish and every skipped phase with the reason, and every phase, gate, step and QA report names it, since a rail cannot tell a skip from a missed report. The live session shows one rail for its current pipeline; resuming it marks done what earlier sessions' kept logs finished.

**Files decide state; the parts meet only in the observer.** Status is rebuilt on every event and page load from the project's files, session logs, each session's liveness and the board, all handed to it: files decide current state, events add who, when, history and stages, and liveness is never re-judged. The observer never guesses the board's location; the board is the kanban folder the project's brain config declares, read through the one brain-config reader, under the kanban tag from the newest session-start report. Records, board and status never depend on each other, so a failure empties only its own part.

## Good examples

**A crashed session found by the next one.** A build session's terminal crashes mid-step and no end hook fires; forty minutes later it reads quiet and maybe stuck. A new session reports its start and calls start, which halts naming that session, its pipeline and the Build phase. The user approves ending it; it is marked ended with its log kept, and the new session resumes the pipeline with Spec through Plan already done on its rail.

**Observer killed mid-session.** A report misses the observer. The agent restarts it once; the first port is now held by another program, so it takes the next on the list and records it. The new observer replays every log, the missed report included, and the next link leads to the new port.

**An open session that never reported.** The user has a plain Claude session open in the project, waiting on them since the morning, that never ran the pipeline. A new pipeline session halts naming it by its host session id as waiting, quiet and maybe stuck, rather than letting two sessions edit one project.

## Bad examples

**Liveness from report age.** A session that exited still counts as live because its last report is recent, blocking the next session; or one waiting on the user past the limit is judged ended and a second session is let in over its unapproved plan.

**Halt from a fresh observer's memory.** The observer was just restarted and has replayed nothing, so start sees no live session and lets a second session proceed while presence on disk shows the first one working.

**Running unobserved.** The observer dies and the restart finds every port busy; the agent shrugs because reports are best effort and builds on, so the user's one source of truth shows a stale session while work lands in the project.

